67752.sol:
The radius of the $\mathrm{n}^{\text{th }}$ Bohr orbit is, $r_n \propto n^2$.
The angular velocity of the electron, $\omega_n \propto \frac{1}{n^3}$
Also, current $I_n=\frac{q}{T_n}=\frac{q \omega_n}{2 \pi}
\begin{array}{ll}
\therefore \quad \mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{n}} \propto \omega_{\mathrm{n}} \propto \frac{1}{\mathrm{n}^3}\\ \text{ Now, } \mathrm{B}_{\mathrm{n}}=\frac{\mu_0 \mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{n}}}{2 \mathrm{r}_{\mathrm{n}}}\\
\therefore \quad \mathrm{B}_{\mathrm{n}} \propto \frac{1}{\mathrm{n}^3} \times \frac{1}{\mathrm{n}^2}\\
\therefore \quad \mathrm{B}_{\mathrm{n}} \propto \frac{1}{\mathrm{n}^5}
\end{array}$

